 {
  "id": "kangaroo-ca-2016-g1112-29",
  "bank": "kangaroo",
  "source": {
   "contest": "Canadian Math Kangaroo",
   "paper": "Grade 11-12",
   "year": 2016,
   "url": "https://api.mathkangaroo.ca/api/v1/media/2016/2016gr1112e.pdf",
   "number": 29
  },
  "grade": "11-12",
  "points": 5,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "We have boxes numbered as 1, 2, 3, … We put a ball with the number 1 into the box number 1. We put two balls numbered 2 and 3 into the box number 2. We put three balls numbered 4, 5 and 6 into the box number 3. And so on. What is the box number containing ball 2016?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "50"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "53"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "60"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "63"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "70"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Triangular numbers",
    "body": "Boxes $1, \\dots, k$ contain $1 + 2 + \\dots + k = \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}$ balls, so the last ball in box $k$ is number $\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}$. For $k = 63$: $\\frac{63 \\cdot 64}{2} = 2016$. So ball 2016 is the last ball in box 63.\n\n**Answer: (D) 63**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Estimate",
    "body": "We need the smallest $k$ with $\\frac{k(k+1)}{2} \\ge 2016$, i.e. $k(k+1) \\ge 4032$. Since $63 \\cdot 64 = 4032$ exactly and $62 \\cdot 63 = 3906 < 4032$, box 62 ends at ball 1953 and box 63 contains balls 1954–2016.\n\n**Answer: (D) 63**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
